---
import { sparcConstraints, sparcIngestion, sparcNumbers } from '../../data/sparc';
---

<section id="sparc">
  <h2>SPARC</h2>
  <p class="secondary">
    Sole engineer, within STARI · Rust backend, Nuxt 4 web app, infrastructure
  </p>
  <p>
    Sensor Platform for Academic Research and Community. In production since January 2026 on two ULB
    campuses, used by a research center (CIREM) and ULB's infrastructure department.
    {sparcNumbers}
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The problem:</strong> research and campus teams need reliable sensor data, but devices
    speak different protocols and no team should have to run its own pipeline.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a href="https://sparc.ulb.be">sparc.ulb.be</a>
  </p>

  <h3>Constraints</h3>
  <ul>
    {sparcConstraints.map((constraint) => (
      <li>{constraint}</li>
    ))}
  </ul>

  <h3>Write and read paths</h3>
  <figure>
    <a href="/sparc-architecture.svg">
      <img
        src="/sparc-architecture.svg"
        width="1120"
        height="590"
        alt="Write path: LoRaWAN sensors through ChirpStack v4, MQTT 5.0 devices through Mosquitto over TLS, and HTTP devices with one API key each, into the Rust ingestion adapters, then NATS JetStream with a dead-letter queue, idempotent Rust consumers and TimescaleDB. Read path: users open the Nuxt 4 web app on OKD, sign in with Authentik (OIDC) and call the Rust query API, which checks tokens against the JWKS, applies RBAC and reads TimescaleDB continuous aggregates. The backend also sends threshold alerts by email, runs asynchronous CSV exports, derives device status from last activity and manages the LoRaWAN fleet. Prometheus and Grafana monitor the platform."
      />
    </a>
    <figcaption>Blue boxes are parts of the single Rust backend service.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3>How data gets in</h3>
  <dl>
    {sparcIngestion.map((item) => (
      <>
        <dt>{item.name}</dt>
        <dd>{item.description}</dd>
      </>
    ))}
  </dl>
</section>
